  "worked_solution": "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-3y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202{f}_{1}}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(4x(1-2x)-xy)=4-16x-y,\\, & \\frac{\u2202{f}_{1}}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(4x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{\u2202{f}_{2}}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(-3y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{\u2202{f}_{2}}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(-3y+2xy)=-3+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}4-16x-y & -x \\\\ 2y & -3+2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{\u2217}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{\u2217})=[\\begin{aligned}4-16(0.5)-0 & -0.5 \\\\ 2(0) & -3+2(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -0.5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{\u00a0and\u00a0}{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real and both negative, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$",
